7: ### What is Gibco MEM Non-Essential Amino Acids?\nL188: MEM Non-Essential Amino Acids are used as a supplement for cell culture medium, to increase cell growth and viability. MEM Non-Essential Amino Acids contains the same non-essential amino acids found in the standard Minimum Essential Medium (MEM) at a strength of 100X. The complete formulation is available here (http://www.thermofisher.com/us/en/home/technical-resources/media-formulation.165.html).\nL189: Find additional tips, troubleshooting help, and resources within our 【59†Cell Culture Support Center】.\nL190: ## Citations & References (12)\nL191: \nL192: Citations & References\nL193: \nL194: Abstract\nL195: \nL196: Identification of a novel redox-sensitive gene, Id3, which mediates angiotensin II-induced cell growth.\nL197: \nL198: Authors:Mueller Cornelius; Baudler Stephanie; Welzel Hilke; BÃƒÂ¶hm Michael; Nickenig Georg;\nL199: \nL200: Journal:Circulation\nL201: \nL202: PubMed ID:12021231\nL203: BACKGROUND: Reactive oxygen species, such as superoxide (O(2)(-)), are involved in the abnormal growth of various cell types. Angiotensin II (Ang II) is one of the most potent inducers of oxidative stress in the vasculature. The molecular events involved in Ang II-induced proliferation of vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs) are ... More\nL204: \nL205: Patient-derived glioblastoma organoids as real-time avatars for assessing responses to clinical CAR-T cell therapy\nL206: Authors:Meghan Logun, Xin Wang, Yusha Sun, Stephen J Bagley, Nannan Li, Arati Desai, Daniel Y Zhang, MacLean P Nasrallah, Emily Ling-Lin Pai, Bike Su Oner, Gabriela Plesa, Donald Siegel, Zev A Binder, Guo-Li Ming, Hongjun Song, Donald M O'Rourke\nL207: \nL208: Journal:Cell Stem Cell\nL209: \nL210: PubMed ID:39657679\nL211: Patient-derived tumor organoids have been leveraged for disease modeling and preclinical studies but rarely applied in real time to aid with interpretation of patient treatment responses in clinics. We recently demonstrated early efficacy signals in a first-in-human, phase 1 study of dual-targeting chimeric antigen receptor (CAR)-T cells (EGFR-IL13Rα2 CAR-T cells) ... More\nL212: Effects of an indole derivative on cell proliferation, transfection, and alternative splicing in production of lentiviral vectors by transient co-transfection.\nL213: \nL214: Authors:Mier NC,Roper DK\nL215: \nL216: Journal:PloS one\nL217: \nL218: PubMed ID:38833479\nL219: Lentiviral vectors derived from human immunodeficiency virus type I are widely used to deliver functional gene copies to mammalian cells for research and gene therapies. Post-transcriptional splicing of lentiviral vector transgene in transduced host and transfected producer cells presents barriers to widespread application of lentiviral vector-based therapies. The present study ... More\nL220: Mutation of the cytoplasmic domain of the integrin beta 3 subunit. Differential effects on cell spreading, recruitment to adhesion plaques, endocytosis, and phagocytosis.\nL221: \nL222: Authors: YlÃ¤nne J; Huuskonen J; O'Toole T E; Ginsberg M H; Virtanen I; Gahmberg C G;\nL223: \nL224: Journal:J Biol Chem\nL225: \nL226: PubMed ID:7721884\nL227: 'The cytoplasmic domain of the beta subunit of the alpha IIb beta 3 integrin is required for cell spreading on fibrinogen. Here we report that deletion of six amino acids from the COOH terminus of the beta 3 (I757TYRGT) totally abolished cell spreading and formation of adhesion plaques, whereas retaining ... More\nL228: \nL229: Gene correction of the apolipoprotein (Apo) E2 phenotype to wild-type ApoE3 by in situ chimeraplasty.\nL230: \nL231: Authors:Tagalakis AD, Graham IR, Riddell DR, Dickson JG, Owen JS,\nL232: \nL233: Journal:J Biol Chem\nL234: PubMed ID:11278248\nL235: \nL236: 'Apolipoprotein (apo) E is a polymorphic plasma protein, synthesized mainly by liver. Here, we evaluate whether synthetic DNA-RNA oligonucleotides (chimeraplasts) can convert a dysfunctional isoform, apoE2 (C --> T, R158C), which causes Type III hyperlipidemia and premature atherosclerosis, into apoE3. First, we treated recombinant Chinese hamster ovary cells stably secreting ... More\nL237: \nL238: 【60†View all MEM Non-Essential Amino Acids Solution (100X) citations】\nL239: \nL240: Ordering\nL241:   * 【50†Order Status】\nL242:   * 【33†Order Help】\nL243:   * 【49†Quick Order】\nL244:   * 【61†Supply Center】\nL245:   * 【62†eProcurement】\nL246: \nL247: Support\nL248: \nL249:   * 【38†Help and Support】\nL250:   * 【48†Contact Us】\nL251:   * 【37†Technical Support Centers】\nL252:   * 【51†Documents and Certificates】\nL253:   * Report a Site Issue\nL254: \nL255: Resources\nL256: \nL257:   * 【35†Learning Centers】\nL258:   * 【52†Promotions】\nL259:   * 【63†Events and Webinars】\nL260:   * 【64†Social Media】\nL261: \nL262: About Thermo Fisher\nL263:   * 【65†About Us†corporate.thermofisher.com】\nL264:   * 【66†Careers†jobs.thermofisher.com】\nL265:   * 【67†Investors†ir.thermofisher.com】\nL266:   * 【68†News†newsroom.thermofisher.com】\nL267:   * 【69†Social Responsibility†corporate.thermofisher.com】\nL268:   * 【70†Trademarks】\nL269:   * 【71†Consumer Health Data Privacy Policy†thermofisher.mastercontrol.com】\nL270:   * 【72†Policies and Notices】\nL271: \nL272: Our Portfolio\nL273:   * 【73†Thermo Scientific】\nL274:   * 【74†Applied Biosystems】\nL275:   * 【75†Invitrogen】\nL276:   * 【76†Gibco】\nL277:   * 【77†Ion Torrent】\nL278:   * 【78†Fisher Scientific】\nL279:   * 【31†Unity Lab Services】\nL280:   * 【23†Patheon】\nL281:   * 【79†PPD】\nL282: \nL283:   * 【80†Terms & Conditions】\nL284:   * 【81†Privacy Notice】\nL285:   * 【82†Price & Freight Policy】\nL286:   * © 2006-2026 Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc. All rights reserved. All trademarks are the property of Thermo Fisher Scientific and its subsidiaries unless otherwise specified.\nL287: \nL288: 【83†Image: United States flag icon】\nL289: \nL290: United States\n"
